ve oxygen species (ROS)** —metabolic “exhaust” that causes oxidative stress and aging. Your body has antioxidant systems (like glutathione) to clean this up, but a massive, fast glucose hit from soda overwhelms those defenses, creating a burst of oxidative damage that damages blood vessels and nerves. A slow-digesting bean provides a steady drip that your cleanup systems handle easily. ### 3. The signaling system isn’t just about “energy”—it’s about a whole-body strategy Your body uses the very presence of different nutrients as hormonal signals. Food isn’t just inert fuel; it’s information. When you eat a huge dose of carbohydrate (especially without fiber), your insulin screams, “STORE FAT! STOP BURNING IT!” This hormonal state dictates what happens to every other calorie you eat. A meal of fat and protein tells a completely different story: “Burn fat, build muscle, stay full.” These signals determine your body composition, not just the final tally of ATP. ### 4. You can’t separate “converting to energy” from organ function If it was all just about ATP, you could live on pure glucose and a multivitamin. But the organs that convert that glucose into energy—your liver and pancreas—would be destroyed in the process. Excess fructose from soda gives the liver fatty deposits and causes insulin resistance, even if it eventually gives you energy. The organ damage *is* the consequence of the process, not the final product. So, while it’s true that all roads eventually lead to some form of usable fuel, one road is a pristine highway that lets your engine run for decades, and the other is a muddy off-road trail that destroys your suspension, overheats the engine, and leaves you stranded by age 50. **The fact that both roads get you to the same city doesn’t mean the trip didn’t matter—the trip *is* your health.**
