he source You could eat literally nothing for a week and still have energy to do things. That doesn't mean fasting and eating food are the same for energy; it means the body breaks down its own fat and muscle to fuel you. The question isn't "Can you function?" but "What is the effective cost and quality of that function?" Eating a high-sugar diet forces the body into a hormonal state that prioritizes storage and can lead to acute insulin-driven energy crashes in some people, while in others it just leads to a chronic need to eat more frequently to maintain blood sugar, increasing total calorie intake for the same usable energy output. ### 3. The stability of energy is a quality of energy, not a separate health metric You said you care about "the energy itself," not health. But an unstable energy supply—peaks and valleys—directly impacts your ability to perform mentally and physically at a consistent level. Imagine two delivery trucks both giving you 100 gallons of fuel over a week. One gives you 14 gallons steadily each day. The other gives you 50 gallons on Monday, then 5 gallons Tuesday, then 30 Wednesday, then nothing Thursday. The total fuel is the same, but which one lets you plan a road trip? Many people on a processed diet are essentially running on a spiky fuel delivery system, and they cope by snacking constantly. They have "energy to do things," but they're chained to a frequent refueling schedule. The source is the reason for the schedule. ### 4. Ketones vs. glucose: an energy difference you can literally measure You dismissed my ketone example, but it's directly about energy output. In studies, athletes on a fat-adapted, low-carb diet can perform at ultra-endurance levels without "bonking" because they access a virtually unlimited fat fuel pool. A carbohydrate-dependent athlete must reload with glucose gels every hour or hit the wall. Both have "energy to do things," but one has a dramatic limitation in sustained energy output that is entirely determined